@@s001 Linear classifier and logistic regression
@@s002 dato:class +1,due:class −1,ora:least squares line,ok:line chosen by hand
@@s003 The data of the notebook (seed 42): 40 points of class +1 around (1, 1) and 40 of class −1 around (−1, −1). The clouds overlap a little.
@@s004 Least squares on the labels ±1: w = [0.052, 0.386, 0.321]. The boundary is the line w₀ + w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ = 0, that is x₂ = −0.16 − 1.20·x₁. Accuracy <b>0.8875</b>: 9 points out of 80 on the wrong side.
@@s005 On one side wᵀx &gt; 0 and the class is <b>+1</b>, on the other −1. A point is classified well if y · f(x) &gt; 0.
@@s006 The line chosen by hand, w = [0.5, 1, 1] (x₂ = −0.5 − x₁), does better: <b>0.925</b>. Least squares does not count the errors: it minimises the distance from ±1, also of the points already well classified.
@@s007 ok:w = 0.15 b = 3 (notebook),due:w = 0.05,ora:w = 0.5,dato:b = −3,no:boundary σ = 0.5
@@s008 The parameters of the notebook: σ(3 + 0.15x). The curve goes from 0 to 1; it is 0.5 where 3 + 0.15x = 0, that is x = <b>−20</b>: the decision boundary.
@@s009 <b>Small w</b> (0.05): the curve is almost flat, similar to a line; the boundary moves to −3/0.05 = −60.
@@s010 <b>Large w</b> (0.5): almost a step, like sign; boundary −3/0.5 = −6.
@@s011 Changing the <b>bias</b> (b = −3 instead of 3) moves the curve without changing its shape: boundary +20.
@@s012 It is read as a probability: with the parameters of the notebook, at x = 0 σ(3) ≈ 0.95, that is a 95 per cent probability of class 1.
@@s013 dato:class +1,due:class −1,ok:boundary wᵀx = 0
@@s014 dato:class B,due:class A,ora:new point and its neighbours
@@s015 Linear classifier and logistic regression
@@s016 Lecture 9 and the exercises of week 10 (<i>Classification and decision boundaries</i>, <i>Introduction to logistic regression</i>). From regression to classification: the same inner product wᵀx, then an activation function that decides the class. Exam question <b>Q8 (a) and (b)</b>. DW chapter 14.
@@s017 Classifying instead of predicting a number
@@s018 <b>Regression</b>: the output is a number (the price of a house, the position on the screen).
@@s019 <b>Classification</b>: the output is a <b>class</b> (cat / not cat, spam / not spam). Binary: two classes, labels y ∈ {−1, 1} (or {0, 1}).
@@s020 The linear (in fact affine) classifier
@@s021 The same inner product as in regression, with x in homogeneous coordinates [1, x₁, x₂]. With w₀ it is affine, even though it is called "linear".
@@s022 The <b>activation</b> function sign turns the number into a class. (For regression the activation is the identity: z itself.)
@@s023 A line in 2D, a plane in 3D, a hyperplane in n dimensions. On one side +1, on the other −1. The weights decide the slope (w₁/w₂) and the position (w₀).
@@s024 Task 2: if label and prediction have the same sign the product is positive (1·1 or −1·−1); if they are different it is −1.
@@s025 wᵀx, sign and the decision boundary
@@s026 Learning w with least squares (Task 4)
@@s027 The decision boundary on the data of the notebook
@@s028 learn_affine_classifier (verified with the data of the notebook, seed 42)
@@s029 The data of the two classes one under the other, and the labels in the same order. Watch out: in the notebook the comment says "-1 for positive class, 0 for negative class", but the text asks for <b>1 and −1</b>: the text counts.
@@s030 Design matrix with the column of 1s (the bias w₀) and least squares: one looks for the w that makes wᵀx as close as possible to ±1.
@@s031 Class = sign. Accuracy 0.8875 (the notebook prints 0.887).
@@s032 Task 5: why by hand it does better (0.925)
@@s033 Least squares does not count the class errors: it measures how far wᵀx is from ±1. A point classified very well, with wᵀx = 1.62, "errs" just the same by (1.62 − 1)² = 0.38 and pulls the boundary towards itself. That is why w = [0.5, 1, 1], chosen by eye, reaches 0.925. For classification one needs losses designed for the purpose: the logistic one (below) or the hinge of the SVMs.
@@s034 Logistic regression
@@s035 Three pieces (Tasks 1–3)
@@s036 A linear function of the input.
@@s037 The <b>sigmoid</b> squeezes any number between 0 and 1: it is read as the <b>probability</b> that x is of class 1.
@@s038 σ(0) = 0.5: the decision boundary is again w₀ + w₁x = 0, the same as the linear classifier. With the parameters of the notebook [3, 0.15]: x = −3/0.15 = <b>−20</b> (σ(−20·0.15 + 3) = 0.5, verified).
@@s039 If you change…
@@s040 the sigmoid…
@@s041 small w₁
@@s042 it becomes almost flat, similar to a line: the probabilities change slowly
@@s043 large w₁
@@s044 it becomes almost a step: it approaches sign
@@s045 it is constant: the probability does not depend on x
@@s046 the bias w₀
@@s047 it shifts to the right or to the left, that is the boundary moves
@@s048 How the parameters change the sigmoid
@@s049 How it is trained: the logistic loss (log loss, binary cross-entropy)
@@s050 For a point of class 1 only −log(p) counts.
@@s051 Little loss if you are sure and right, a great deal if you are sure and wrong. Unlike least squares, a point "very right" is not punished.
@@s052 The sigmoid makes the problem non-linear in the weights: one starts from a w and improves it in small steps (week 14). With the data of Task 4, sklearn's <code>LogisticRegression</code> finds w₀ = 0.205, w = [1.791, 1.537], accuracy 0.8875.
@@s053 Why probabilities are useful
@@s054 A probability also says <b>how</b> sure the model is. Changing the threshold 0.5 exchanges false positives and false negatives (week 11): in medicine one lowers the threshold so as not to miss sick people.
@@s055 Accuracy 0.04 in a two-class problem
@@s056 In Task 5 of the notebook "accuracy 0.040" appears. With two classes, an accuracy far below 0.5 almost always means <b>inverted labels</b> (the model says 1 where the truth is 0): the classifier is excellent (0.96) but compared with the swapped classes.
@@s057 k-nearest neighbour (lecture 9)
@@s058 A new point takes the class of the <b>majority of its k neighbours</b> in the training. It learns no weights: it remembers all the data (memory-based) and measures distances, so the choice of the <a href="w02-norme-distanze.html">distance</a> and the scale of the features matter. k = 1 gives accuracy 1.0 on the training (each point is its own neighbour): it says nothing about generalisation. Pros: no training, boundaries of any shape. Cons: slow to predict with many data, sensitive to unscaled features and to useless ones.
@@s059 Q8 (a) and (b)
@@s060 (a) "Linear classification, kernels, and classification boundaries." (b) "Logistic regression and classification boundaries." To bring: wᵀx and the sign, the boundary as a hyperplane, y·f(x) &gt; 0, least squares on the labels and its limit (Task 5), sigmoid and probability, boundary at σ = 0.5, log loss and gradient descent.
@@s061 k = 1, 3, 5: the answer changes
